Third-party components in or used by Scion's kernel (a fork of HakiOS)
(full table with origins and status: THIRD_PARTY_LICENSES.md; compliance notes: COMPLIANCE.md)

Where this tree comes from:

- HakiOS (github.com/oosexgang/hakios-xnu-kernel and its sibling repositories, by the HakiOS authors): Scion's kernel is a fork of
  it. The tree, the XNU patch, the loader, user space, tools and device layer started there; "Copyright (c) 2025-2026 the HakiOS
  authors" in LICENSE covers that work, under the licences by directory that LICENSE sets out. Scion's changes are under the same
  licences. The HakiOS authors' notices stay in the files they belong to.

In the repository:

- XNU (Apple): the kernel sources are fetched by xnu/apply.sh from github.com/apple-oss-distributions/xnu;
  our patch and files are a derivative work under the Apple Public Source License 2.0 (LICENSE.APSL).
- ios-on-pixel-10 (TheAndersMadsen; Scion-Project/scion-blazer): devices/blazer/carried/ holds kernel-side files that are its
  author's own, MIT by their headers ("Copyright (c) 2026 hackios contributors" as the source states it), used in Scion with
  the author's permission and his credit kept. Its third-party parts (ios-tegu-derived code, which has no licence; GPL-2.0-only
  UFS link code; GPL-derived panel tables) are not in this repository: docs/two-bases.md.
- libdeflate (Eric Biggers), scion/xnu-8796:bsd/miscfs/apfs/libdeflate/: MIT, see its COPYING.
- Monocypher 4.0.2 (Loup Vaillant and others), scion/xnu-8796:osfmk/minicrypto/third_party/monocypher.*: CC0-1.0 or BSD-2-Clause.
- BearSSL (Thomas Pornin), scion/xnu-8796:osfmk/minicrypto/third_party/bearssl_*: MIT.
- SoftwareAES (UniXNU, ArmVirtPlatform contributors), github.com/UniXNU/SoftwareAES at 4a45326da5c2:
  scion/xnu-8796:iokit/Kernel/MiniIOAESAccelerator.cpp is a port of it, under BSD-2-Clause-Patent (notice in the file).
- UniXNU (github.com/UniXNU/UniXNU, BSD-2-Clause-Patent): commit 0090 of scion/xnu-12377, the PAC keys in the architectural
  registers on a non-Apple CPU, follows the design of its native-pac-keys.patch; no UniXNU code is copied there. Patches
  0096 (PAR_EL1 ISB), 0097 (64-bit physical address in the pmap's page-data sync) and 0098 (arm64 processes with the PAC keys
  off, behind SCION_NATIVE_PAC) are UniXNU's own small patches (native-at-par-sync, zzx-pmap-sync-64bit-pa,
  native-pac-arm64-user-jop and a hunk of native-pac-keys, at a9ec98c and f65660d/a11553a, by their contributors), taken with
  their notice, LICENSES/BSD-2-Clause-Patent-UniXNU.txt, under BSD-2-Clause-Patent; 0098's macro name is ours.
- mkbootimg.py (Android Open Source Project), devices/redwood/prebuilt/mkbootimg.py: Apache License 2.0.
- Mu-Silicium / Project Mu (Project Silicium, Microsoft and contributors): devices/redwood/uefi/ holds a
  patch and files against it; their license applies to the derived parts (BSD-2-Clause-Patent).
- UEFI driver binaries of the phone's own firmware, devices/redwood/uefi/files/Binaries/redwood/: the
  vendor's (Qualcomm/Xiaomi), extracted from the phone's xbl.img the way Project Silicium redistributes
  such drivers for other phones. They are needed only to rebuild the UEFI image; they can be removed and
  extracted by each user from their own phone's firmware with UEFIReader.

- the Galaxy S9's device repository (Scion-Project/hakios-starlte, checked out at devices/starlte/):
  prebuilt/SILICIUM_UEFI.fd and BootShim.bin are built from Mu-Silicium with the port's changes
  (BSD-2-Clause, BSD-2-Clause-Patent); the UEFI image carries Project Silicium's Carbon Hypervisor
  (Hyp.bin, GPL-2.0, source at github.com/Project-Silicium/Carbon-Hypervisor); prebuilt/mkbootimg.py is
  AOSP's (Apache 2.0). The S9's vendor firmware (Samsung's audio, Broadcom's Wi-Fi,
  Bluetooth and sensor hub) is not in any Scion repository since 7 Nov 2026: devices/starlte keeps only its sha256 sums and
  tools/fetch-firmware.sh, which fetches it from the user's own copy or the public LineageOS vendor trees; the vendor's,
  redistribution terms "to verify" (THIRD_PARTY_LICENSES.md).

- the FocalTech touch firmware, drivers/input/touch/hakios.focaltech_fw.h: the touch vendor's;
  generated from a device blob, redistribution terms "to verify" (THIRD_PARTY_LICENSES.md).

- the Wi-Fi driver: Linux's ath11k (BSD-3-Clause-Clear) and FreeBSD's LinuxKPI headers (BSD-2-Clause) are
  fetched by tools/mk-ath11k.sh at pinned releases and built on our layer wifi/lx (MIT); only the layer is here.
- the Galaxy S9's Wi-Fi driver: Linux's brcmfmac and brcmutil (ISC), of the same pinned release, are fetched by
  tools/mk-brcmfmac.sh, patched with wifi/lx/brcmfmac/patches/ (ISC, as the files it changes) and built on the same
  layer (wifi/lx/brcmfmac, MIT); only the layer and the patch are here.

- the community Galaxy S9+ port (danstronics/ios-s9plus-exynos, a Scion-based port; its LICENSE: MIT for its own layer, "the HakiOS
  authors"): scion/xnu-8796:bsd/sys/hakios.touch_queue.h and tools/touch-queue-test.c are from its core/drivers/input, and the
  touch_coalesce argument in drivers/input/hakios.input.c is that behaviour; tools/mesa/panfrost-mapping-cache.patch is its
  baseline-support/panfrost-cache-mapping.patch (a patch to Mesa 25.0.7: Mesa's licence applies to the lines it adds). Each file's
  header names the source. docs/star2lte-absorb.md says what else of it was read and left. Nothing Apple's or the vendor's.
- Mesa: tools/mesa/*.patch are patches to Mesa (MIT, per-file licences), applied by tools/mk-mesa.sh to the Mesa a build fetches;
  only the patches are here, and each is under the licence of the file it changes.
- Android boot image header (xnuloader/chooser.h, CHOOSER=1): the format is AOSP's bootimg.h (Apache-2.0); the parser is written
  from the format, no AOSP code is copied. The flattened device tree edit is written from the Devicetree Specification.
- Facts, not code, from the Starbite loader for the Galaxy S9 (GPL-2.0-only): the GPA0 key register and bit, and the three RAM
  addresses of the Android handoff, in xnuloader/chooser.h and chooser.c. None of its code or text is here.

- AdrenoMetal (UniXNU, "NativeMetal contributors"), userland/src/metalvk/NativeBridgeMetalFrontend.* and
  tools/metal/bridge/NativeMetal*: MIT, see LICENSE.AdrenoMetal beside them; the Metal-on-Vulkan plug-in (METAL=vk).

- Project Starbite's Metal 2 frontend and bridge, tools/ios27/metal2/ (HotMetal2.mm, NativeBridgeMetalFrontend.*, bridge/): MIT, the
  Starbite author's, from IOS-26-Hot (dipa-studio, MIT, LICENSE.IOS-26-Hot and bridge/LICENSE beside them) and, before it, UniXNU's
  AdrenoMetal (NativeMetal contributors, MIT). Its bridge/patches are patches to metal2vulkan, under metal2vulkan's licence.

- patches to Mesa, tools/mesa/*.patch, and the libdrm stand-in tools/mesa/hakios-drm/: Mesa's and X.Org's MIT terms.
- patches to UniXNU's AdrenoMetal, tools/m2v/adrenometal-ios16.patch: MIT, Copyright (c) 2026 NativeMetal contributors.
- a patch to steelbrain's metal2vulkan, tools/m2v/metal2vulkan-hakios.patch: GPL-2.0-or-later OR LGPL-3.0-or-later, as the code it
  changes, not MIT.
- userland/src/HakiosMetalCA.m: begun from vcvkk's plug-in (github.com/vcvkk/darwin-redwood, branch hakios-workspace, at eb9fdf5),
  with this tree's changes. That origin's license is "to verify" (THIRD_PARTY_LICENSES.md).

Fetched at build time, never committed:

- the Darwin userland 16.5 IPSW (Apple): the user space of the system image; the user gets it and
  sysimage/unpack.sh unpacks it on their machine; nothing downloads it; its license is Apple's.
- the iPhoneOS 16.5 SDK from github.com/theos/sdks, and theos's cctools (Apple's open-source cctools): the
  kernel's and user space's build tools.
- the phone's firmware (GPU, Wi-Fi, audio DSP, modem, haptics, cameras): the vendor's, pulled from the
  user's own phone (devices/redwood/pull-firmware.sh).
- lzfse (Apple, BSD-3-Clause), built by xnuloader/mkidentity.sh when no LZFSE decoder is installed.
- Mesa (for GL=1 images; Turnip for TURNIP=1, tools/mk-turnip.sh), Procursus and Sileo packages (tools/mk-jb.sh): their own licenses.
- Mesa's PanVK (Mesa 25.0.7, MIT), for PANVK_DIR= images: fetched by tools/mk-panvk.sh, patched with tools/mesa/*.patch
  and tools/mesa/panvk/*.patch (Mesa's licence applies to the lines those add) and built into libvulkan_panfrost.dylib.
- hakios-metal (oosexgang, MIT), in hakios-metal/ of this tree with its own LICENSE and NOTICE, built by `make ios` there and
  linked by userland/build.sh METAL_PLUGIN=ca (HAKIOS_METAL= names another copy).
- for M2V=1, by tools/mk-m2v.sh, at pinned revisions: UniXNU's AdrenoMetal 243d63c (MIT, Copyright (c) 2026 NativeMetal contributors);
  steelbrain's metal2vulkan 624bab3 (GPL-2.0-or-later OR LGPL-3.0-or-later) with AdrenoMetal's 30 patches; the Rust crates of its
  Cargo.lock (MIT, Apache-2.0, ISC, Unlicense: their own licenses). None of it is in prebuilt/. A binary that statically links that
  runtime has obligations, which are "to verify" before any distribution (THIRD_PARTY_LICENSES.md, COMPLIANCE.md).
- QuartzCore's default.metallib for the window server's Metal (tools/ca-metallib.py): Apple's; what you supply from your own Mac or IPSW,
  on your machine, never committed.
- metal2vulkan (github.com/steelbrain/metal2vulkan; GPL-2.0-or-later OR LGPL-3.0-or-later), for METAL=vk images:
  fetched and patched by tools/metal/mk-metal2vulkan.sh and built only into libmetal2vulkan.dylib, a library of its own
  that the plug-in links and that can be replaced. None of its code is in this repository's files but our changes to it,
  patch files in tools/metal/m2v/, which are under its licence and say so in their headers.

- Project Starbite (its author, credited as "Starbite author" until he names the account he wants), the iOS 27 port to the Galaxy S9
